---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { gradleDependencies, SEMPODS_VERSION } from '../data/coordinates';

const readQuery = `curl -X POST https://sempods.org/aaltra/_system/sparql/query \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/sparql-query" \\
  -H "Accept: application/sparql-results+json" \\
  --data 'PREFIX schema: <https://schema.org/>
SELECT ?e ?name ?start WHERE {
  ?e a schema:Event ; schema:name ?name ; schema:startDate ?start
} ORDER BY ?start LIMIT 3'`;
---

<Base
  title="Start with sempods — query a pod, read the specification, run your own"
  description="Start with sempods by querying a live public pod, reading the specification, running your own pod, or building against the published JVM artifacts."
  schemaType="TechArticle"
>
  <p class="eyebrow">try it yourself</p>
  <h1>Start with the part that is real today</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Run the query in step 1. It takes ten seconds, needs nothing installed, and
    it is the fastest thing here that proves something instead of asserting it.
  </p>

  <p>
    After that the ladder forks by what brought you here, and the rungs are not
    equally polished. If you want to know whether the model holds up, step 3 is
    the specification and the rest is optional. If you want to build something,
    step 7 is the artifacts. If you want a pod of your own, step 4 is the fast
    way and step 5 is the honest one.
  </p>

  <section class="try-block">
    <h2>1. Query a live public pod</h2>

    <p>
      Public contexts are readable without authentication. This query reads a
      real venue's event calendar from its own pod:
    </p>

    <pre><code>{readQuery}</code></pre>

    <p class="evidence">
      The endpoint is ordinary HTTP. The query is ordinary SPARQL. The result is
      JSON from live linked data, not a special website API.
    </p>
  </section>

  <h2>2. Follow the data</h2>

  <p>
    The query returns event URIs. Open one and the pod returns the resource
    itself with the statements you are allowed to see. That is the Linked Data
    part: the identifier is also the address.
  </p>

  <p>
    This is the smallest useful proof of decentralization. The venue owns the
    event graph. Apps and websites can use it without becoming the place where
    the data lives.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Read the specification</h2>

  <p>
    Two steps of live data prove that one server behaves a certain way. The{' '}
    <a href="https://spec.sempods.org">specification</a> is the claim that this is
    a contract rather than one server's habits: core chapters for contexts,
    grants, authorization, CRUD, SPARQL and <code>find</code>, optional modules
    beside them, and every normative statement carrying an identifier so it can
    be cited instead of paraphrased.
  </p>

  <p>
    The contract is intentionally built from known standards. A new implementation
    should not need a new graph database, a new auth framework or a new query
    engine before it can begin. It needs to make the profiled stack agree on the
    sempods rules: one pod base URL, one context per statement, grants resolved
    by the server, the same sandbox across HTTP and SPARQL, and the same rule for
    MCP when that optional module is implemented.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <a href="https://spec.sempods.org/api/">HTTP surface</a> is the same
    thing with a request button: the OpenAPI descriptions rendered as a client.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is <em>descriptive</em> until <code>0.1</code> is tagged — it is being
    extracted from the reference implementation, and where the two disagree the
    implementation is right. Read it as an accurate description of something that
    runs, not yet as a promise it will not move.
  </p>

  <p class="action-links">
    <a href="https://spec.sempods.org">Read the specification</a>
    <a href="https://spec.sempods.org/api/">Open the HTTP surface</a>
  </p>

  <h2>4. Ask for a hosted test pod</h2>

  <p>
    Concrete pods can already live under sempods.org paths such as{' '}
    <code>https://sempods.org/aaltra</code>. A pod base URL is an address, not a
    page: nothing is served at the root of it today, so the shortest readable
    proof that a pod is there is its{' '}
    <a href="https://sempods.org/aaltra/_system/contexts">context list</a>, which
    an anonymous caller sees as exactly one public context. There is no public
    hosting entry UI yet; new pods for early ideas or tests are created manually
    on request.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is the fastest path when you want a real pod without operating the
    infrastructure yourself: backups, updates and migrations across version or
    schema changes are handled on the hosted side. For a test pod, write to{' '}
    <a href="mailto:danilo@sempods.org">danilo@sempods.org</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Run your own pod</h2>

  <p>
    The stronger decentralization proof is self-hosting. In{' '}
    <a href="https://github.com/sempods/sempods-kotlin">sempods-kotlin</a>, the
    local deployment brings up a pod and the services behind it, so you can test
    against infrastructure you run yourself.
  </p>

  <p>
    This path is rougher than asking for a hosted test pod, but it matters. It
    proves sempods is not one hosted service with a public API; the hosted path
    is only the fastest way to begin.
  </p>

  <p class="action-links">
    <a href="https://github.com/sempods/sempods-kotlin/tree/main/deployments/local">Open local deployment</a>
  </p>

  <h2>6. Read or run the reference implementation</h2>

  <p>
    <a href="https://github.com/sempods/sempods-kotlin">sempods-kotlin</a>{' '}is the
    reference implementation. It contains the working server and client stack:
    Linked Data CRUD, contexts, grants, SPARQL sandboxing and a pod MCP endpoint
    at <code>/_system/mcp</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The aaltra pod exposes that MCP path too, so the same live data can be tested
    through HTTP, SPARQL or MCP without changing the ownership model.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is not meant to be the only implementation — it is the one the{' '}
    <a href="https://spec.sempods.org">specification</a> was extracted from, which
    is a different claim. A second implementation would be written against the
    text; this one is what the text currently describes.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Build against the JVM artifacts</h2>

  <p>
    The artifacts are on Maven Central. Until a compiled quickstart example is
    pulled into the website, the coordinates live in one place here:
  </p>

  <pre><code>{gradleDependencies}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Version {SEMPODS_VERSION}, Java 21 bytecode. <code>SempodsPodClient</code>{' '}
    covers reading, writing, contexts and SPARQL.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Try the preview apps</h2>

  <p>
    <a href="https://apps.sempods.org">apps.sempods.org</a>{' '}is an early
    collection of apps that connect to a pod by URL. Explorer visualizes the
    graph and is the one worth opening first. Focus, a todo app, and Konsum, a
    shopping list, are experimental enough that they are not linked directly from
    here.
  </p>

  <p>
    The surface is still rough, but the architecture is the important part here:
    these apps work against pods through the SDK that is being prepared for
    public release.
  </p>

  <figure class="media-breakout">
    <a
      href="/images/explorer-aaltra.webp"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener"
      aria-label="Open larger Explorer screenshot"
    >
      <img
        src="/images/explorer-aaltra.webp"
        width="1600"
        height="1005"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
        alt="Explorer app visualizing an aaltra event from a sempods pod as a graph"
      />
    </a>
    <figcaption>
      Explorer reads the aaltra pod as a graph: event, description, dates, image
      data and schema.org terms stay connected. The image opens larger for
      reading labels and edges.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p class="action-links app-links">
    <a href="https://apps.sempods.org/explorer/">Open Explorer</a>
    <a href="https://apps.sempods.org">See preview apps</a>
  </p>

  <h2>What is not here yet</h2>

  <p>
    The public TypeScript SDK is the next important surface for app builders.
    Once it is open, this page should shift from "read the reference
    implementation" toward "build a small app against a pod". It is the one rung
    of the ladder that is missing rather than rough.
  </p>

  <p>
    SHACL shapes and a TBox model may become part of that later: apps and agents
    should eventually be able to discover the shapes and meaning they can work
    with, not rely only on prose and convention.
  </p>

  <hr class="divider" />

  <p>
    Something does not behave as written?
    <a href="https://github.com/sempods/sempods-kotlin/issues">Open an issue</a>.
    A first-run problem is one of the most useful reports a project at this
    stage can receive.
  </p>

  <p>
    Looking for something this page does not cover — the repositories, the
    vocabulary, the published artifacts, where to ask a question?{' '}
    <a href="/links">Everything sempods is, on one page</a>.
  </p>
</Base>
